-- Exam for the MEPS course, which went live without one. Only fills it if the course still has no questions.
UPDATE "courses" SET "exam_questions" = '[{"id":"meps-q1","type":"choice","text":"What is the main purpose of Minimum Energy Performance Standards (MEPS)?","options":["To set the retail price of appliances","To rank the best brands for consumers","To keep the least efficient products off the market","To ban all imported appliances"],"correctIndex":2},{"id":"meps-q2","type":"choice","text":"How does better energy efficiency help African countries with limited or strained power supply?","options":["It delivers the same service using less electricity, easing pressure on supply","It raises the demand on the grid","It removes the need for any new generation or grid access","It only benefits the manufacturers of appliances"],"correctIndex":0},{"id":"meps-q3","type":"choice","text":"What is AFREC?","options":["A refrigerant trade association","A private appliance testing company","A European funding bank","The African Energy Commission, the African Union''s specialised energy agency"],"correctIndex":3},{"id":"meps-q4","type":"choice","text":"What is the key difference between a MEPS and an energy label?","options":["They are two names for the same rule","A MEPS sets a minimum level a product must meet, while a label informs buyers about its performance","A MEPS applies only to lighting and a label only to cooling","A label is a legal limit and a MEPS is only advice"],"correctIndex":1},{"id":"meps-q5","type":"choice","text":"How does a comparative label differ from an endorsement label?","options":["A comparative label is always voluntary and an endorsement label is always mandatory","A comparative label lets buyers compare similar products on a scale, while an endorsement label is a seal showing set criteria were met","An endorsement label shows a star rating and a comparative label shows no information","There is no difference"],"correctIndex":1},{"id":"meps-q6","type":"choice","text":"Why are MEPS and energy labels often used together?","options":["Labels make MEPS unnecessary after the first year","Together they fix the price of each product","They allow inefficient products to be sold more cheaply","MEPS remove the worst products while labels help buyers choose better ones"],"correctIndex":3},{"id":"meps-q7","type":"choice","text":"Why does a mandatory MEPS need a legal or regulatory basis?","options":["So that an authority has the power to require compliance and act on breaches","So that products can be sold without testing","So that manufacturers can write their own limits","So that consumers must buy a particular brand"],"correctIndex":0},{"id":"meps-q8","type":"choice","text":"What is a main benefit of neighbouring countries harmonising their MEPS?","options":["Each country must build a separate test method","Every product must carry several different labels","It reduces trade barriers and makes it harder to dump inefficient products in the country with weaker rules","Prices are set jointly by the governments"],"correctIndex":2},{"id":"meps-q9","type":"choice","text":"Who should be consulted when a MEPS regulation is being prepared?","options":["Only the energy ministry","Only foreign manufacturers","Nobody, because consultation is not part of the process","Manufacturers, importers, retailers, test laboratories, customs and consumer groups"],"correctIndex":3},{"id":"meps-q10","type":"choice","text":"What is normally the first step in developing a MEPS for a product?","options":["Assessing the market: which products are sold and how efficient they are","Fining importers of the product","Printing the labels","Banning the product until it is tested"],"correctIndex":0},{"id":"meps-q11","type":"choice","text":"Why must a MEPS refer to a defined test standard?","options":["So that the product costs less to make","So that performance is measured the same way and results can be compared and verified","So that each supplier can choose how to measure its own product","So that testing is no longer needed"],"correctIndex":1},{"id":"meps-q12","type":"choice","text":"What does market surveillance do in a MEPS programme?","options":["It advertises efficient products","It sets the price of electricity","It checks that products on sale actually comply with the standard and the label","It trains installers to size systems"],"correctIndex":2},{"id":"meps-q13","type":"choice","text":"Why should MEPS levels be reviewed and tightened from time to time?","options":["Because technology improves and an old minimum stops pushing the market forward","Because labels wear out","Because test laboratories require it to stay open","Because a MEPS legally expires every year"],"correctIndex":0},{"id":"meps-q14","type":"choice","text":"An air conditioner draws 1.5 kW and runs for 1,000 hours a year. How much electricity does it use in a year?","options":["150 kWh","15,000 kWh","1,500 kWh","1,000 kWh"],"correctIndex":2},{"id":"meps-q15","type":"choice","text":"Model A uses 1,500 kWh a year and Model B uses 1,200 kWh a year. Electricity costs US$0.10 per kWh. How much does Model B save over 10 years?","options":["US$30","US$300","US$120","US$3,000"],"correctIndex":1},{"id":"meps-q16","type":"choice","text":"An inspector''s verification test finds that a model on sale does not meet the MEPS. What is the appropriate response?","options":["Change the test result so that the model passes","Lower the MEPS for all products","Ignore it unless consumers complain","Apply the enforcement steps set out in the regulation, such as corrective action, penalties or removal from the market"],"correctIndex":3}]'::jsonb, "updated_at" = now()
WHERE "title" = 'Energy Efficiency & MEPS Framework' AND jsonb_array_length("exam_questions") = 0;
